      "text": "To find a positive integer whose digits add up to 11, let's think about how multiples of 9 can help us here.\n\nMultiples of 9 add up to 18, so, we need a combination of numbers to reach 11.\n\nFirstly, the single digit 9 should be counted as the first number in the sequence. But we need an additional digit. So we will add a two-digit number:\n$$\n10+1+9 = 20\n$$\n\nHowever, 20 is still larger than 11 and doesn't match the given condition. Let's try 5 as the second digit.\n$$\n10+1+5 = 16\n$$\n\nStill too high. Let's use 4 for the second digit:\n$$\n10+1+4 = 15\n$$\n\nThis is closer! Now we have 2015 which is larger than 11 but closer. The sequence starts with 29, we need to find the equivalent number for 297 with the constraint of only having digits 1-9 and adding up to 11.\n\nKeep in mind,\n$$\n297 = (2+9)(9+9+9)\n$$\n\nThen we calculate,\n\\begin{align\\*}\n&(2+9)(9+9+9) = \\\\\n&(9+9+9)(9+9+9) = \\\\\n&409+190=599\n\\end{align\\*}\n\nOur desired positive integer is 99+2=101, and we need 3 spaces. The eleventh number in the sequence must be:\n$$\n297+3=300\n$$\n\nThe eleventh positive integer under the given conditions is thus $ \\boxed{300} $",
